WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.020 --> 00:00:05.680
So, I originally wrote three pages of this script about the Snyder Cut of Justice League in December 2017,

00:00:05.800 --> 00:00:08.720
then shelved it because the writing was going a bit slow,

00:00:08.920 --> 00:00:14.780
and I figured that by the time I got it out it would have blown over, and no one would care anymore.

00:00:15.120 --> 00:00:23.280
Well, now it's December 2019, so let's talk about why the Zack Snyder cut of Justice League both does and does not exist.

00:00:31.100 --> 00:00:33.080
Alright, a bit of background.

00:00:33.120 --> 00:00:38.040
Warner Bros.' Justice League, the last Snyder-helmed entry in the DC Extended Universe,

00:00:38.040 --> 00:00:44.500
and a direct sequel to 2016's Batman v Superman: Dawn of Justice, opened on November 17th 2017

00:00:44.500 --> 00:00:49.240
to largely middling reviews and lukewarm audience reception.

00:00:49.380 --> 00:00:55.480
The film, which cost between 300 and 350 million dollars, excluding promotion,

00:00:55.480 --> 00:00:59.800
underperformed at the box office, surpassing its strict production budget

00:00:59.820 --> 00:01:03.420
with a worldwide box office of a little under 660 million,

00:01:03.420 --> 00:01:08.400
but falling far short of the billion dollar return Warner Bros. was hoping for,

00:01:08.620 --> 00:01:14.010
which would have placed it in the same competitive category as Disney's Star Wars and Avengers films.

00:01:14.010 --> 00:01:19.720
The film has turned a reasonable profit in the long run, between box office and home release,

00:01:19.720 --> 00:01:25.500
but you don't put 350 million dollars into a movie just to get a reasonable profit.

00:01:25.900 --> 00:01:28.880
Ardent fans of the franchise have pinned the film's lack of success

00:01:28.880 --> 00:01:33.380
on a combination of studio meddling and a conspiracy of critics who, fans claim,

00:01:33.380 --> 00:01:36.780
are determined to see the DC Extended Universe fail.

00:01:36.940 --> 00:01:41.800
The first of those is wishful thinking, while the second is pure fantasy.

00:01:41.860 --> 00:01:45.620
Now, the final run of Justice League's development was troubled.

00:01:45.620 --> 00:01:48.920
In May of 2017, following the tragic death of his daughter,

00:01:48.920 --> 00:01:53.820
Zack Snyder withdrew as head of the project to be with family and focus on grieving.

00:01:53.920 --> 00:01:59.140
Simultaneously, Joss Whedon was brought on board to take his place and helm the final delivery of the film.

00:01:59.380 --> 00:02:06.000
While this was initially presented as Joss essentially just running it in and overseeing some minor additional photography

00:02:06.009 --> 00:02:08.669
Snyder had already planned prior to his departure,

00:02:09.129 --> 00:02:13.409
Joss's involvement rapidly ballooned. Rumours had already been circulating that Warner

00:02:13.650 --> 00:02:19.200
Bros. weren't happy with the film, including specific allegations that executives called the film unwatchable

00:02:19.390 --> 00:02:24.509
after a screening in April. The suspicion is that while Snyder stepped back for reasons of personal

00:02:24.819 --> 00:02:29.878
tragedy, this provided the opportunity for executives at Warner Bros. to cut him out entirely.

00:02:30.159 --> 00:02:36.400
This suspicion is supported by the expansion of the additional photography from two weeks to two months,

00:02:36.400 --> 00:02:39.800
with an estimated cost of around twenty five million dollars.

00:02:39.800 --> 00:02:44.000
This additional photography included a particularly high-profile conflict

00:02:44.000 --> 00:02:49.170
with Superman actor Henry Cavill's role on the then-upcoming Universal film Mission Impossible

00:02:49.510 --> 00:02:55.950
Six that required Warner Bros. to digitally remove Cavill's mustache from all the new footage, an effect that is...

00:02:56.530 --> 00:02:57.760
charitably...

00:02:57.760 --> 00:03:03.090
not very good. As an end result, critics and audiences largely agree that the final film

00:03:03.189 --> 00:03:08.399
distinctly feels like an uneven mashup of the two directors' styles. Not a merger,

00:03:08.409 --> 00:03:10.409
but more like two different

00:03:10.569 --> 00:03:14.969
films starring the same actors had just been cut together.

00:03:15.099 --> 00:03:18.988
A particularly aggressive slice of the fandom has, in response started, a petition

00:03:19.060 --> 00:03:22.600
demanding that Warner Bros. release Zack Snyder's version of the movie;

00:03:22.600 --> 00:03:27.000
the film as it was before Whedon was brought on board, under the banner

00:03:27.099 --> 00:03:31.709
"Release The Snyder Cut". Zack Snyder himself has, over the course of 2019,

00:03:32.199 --> 00:03:37.199
increasingly leaned in on the "Release the Snyder Cut" phenomenon, which I, I mean

00:03:37.199 --> 00:03:38.530
it makes sense.

00:03:38.530 --> 00:03:45.400
It directly serves his ego and the fact that Warner Bros. hasn't let him anywhere near a director's chair since 2017,

00:03:45.400 --> 00:03:51.000
to the point that Snyder had to take Army of the Dead to Netflix after a seven-film run with Warners.

00:03:51.200 --> 00:03:55.000
In early December 2019 Zack Snyder, posted a photo on Vero

00:03:55.000 --> 00:03:59.700
of a rack of film canisters labeled "Justice League Director's Cut, running time

00:03:59.799 --> 00:04:04.400
214 minutes," with the overlaid text "Is it real? Does it exist?

00:04:04.400 --> 00:04:06.129
Of course it does."

00:04:06.129 --> 00:04:07.329
So...

00:04:07.329 --> 00:04:10.000
Does the Snyder Cut exist?

00:04:10.000 --> 00:04:13.500
Well, as the image says, of course it does,

00:04:13.569 --> 00:04:17.000
depending on your definition of "exist."

00:04:17.200 --> 00:04:23.459
Now, just to be clear, there is, saved to a hard drive somewhere, a version of the film Justice League

00:04:23.460 --> 00:04:27.200
that could be called "The Zack Snyder Cut." There is a rack of film

00:04:27.490 --> 00:04:31.019
canisters containing a three-and-a-half-hour version of Justice League.

00:04:31.419 --> 00:04:34.000
These are "The Snyder Cut".

00:04:34.200 --> 00:04:38.000
The problem is that it doesn't look like this,

00:04:38.080 --> 00:04:40.400
It looks like this:

00:04:46.110 --> 00:04:49.600
That right there is part of the leaked work print of X-Men Origins: Wolverine,

00:04:49.600 --> 00:04:53.400
a version of the film as it existed about three months before release.

00:04:53.600 --> 00:04:57.800
It is highly instructive for our purposes here because it clearly demonstrates

00:04:57.800 --> 00:05:00.900
the piecemeal nature in which effects-heavy films are finished.

00:05:01.120 --> 00:05:06.360
Films aren't made like Apocalypse Now anymore where, there's a million feet of footage

00:05:06.360 --> 00:05:11.000
the director can endlessly recombine into a more or less finished movie.

00:05:11.200 --> 00:05:15.900
Almost every single frame of Justice League involves some degree of manipulation.

00:05:16.210 --> 00:05:22.949
Elements like sky replacements, fog and particle effects, vehicle replacements background, signs, logos, costumes,

00:05:23.080 --> 00:05:24.730
so on, so forth.

00:05:24.730 --> 00:05:30.659
Now, the petition writers assert that there is a more or less complete Snyder version of the movie,

00:05:30.970 --> 00:05:34.400
stemming from misunderstanding some tweets by Larry Fong

00:05:34.400 --> 00:05:37.000
talking about the digital intermediary process.

00:05:37.200 --> 00:05:42.179
The petition's conspiracy hinges on a misunderstanding of the term "picture lock,"

00:05:42.180 --> 00:05:45.450
so first we need to talk about picture lock.

00:05:45.450 --> 00:05:47.759
It is not the point where the image is complete,

00:05:47.950 --> 00:05:54.689
but the point where these shot choices, shot length, scene order, and core editing are more or less done.

00:05:54.800 --> 00:06:02.190
In practice, It's a very loose term, and the euphemistic phrase "soft lock" is often thrown around on productions.

00:06:02.350 --> 00:06:09.000
It's not a physical state, but a social agreement between departments to stop changing things.

00:06:09.200 --> 00:06:12.520
Though, changing things still happens a lot.

00:06:12.520 --> 00:06:17.000
The point of picture lock is to get the film to a point where other post-production departments,

00:06:17.280 --> 00:06:21.900
like digital graphics, digital effects, Foley sound mixing, and score can start working

00:06:22.030 --> 00:06:27.630
without too much of a risk that their work is going to go to waste, that they're not going to spend a week

00:06:28.030 --> 00:06:33.800
rotoscoping wires just for the shot to be replaced with an alternate take or a different angle once they're done.

00:06:34.000 --> 00:06:36.390
And again, that still happens all the time.

00:06:36.700 --> 00:06:41.200
This all means that picture lock-on effects heavy films tend to roll out in chunks,

00:06:41.200 --> 00:06:44.300
with scenes or even shots being blocked on a rolling

00:06:44.520 --> 00:06:47.400
basis in order to allow the effects team to get to work.

00:06:47.400 --> 00:06:51.400
Since digital effects on films like Justice League can take upwards of two years,

00:06:51.600 --> 00:06:56.800
this even leads to some scenes being edited and locked while the movie is still being shot.

00:06:57.000 --> 00:06:59.370
Again, X-Men Origins is highly instructive.

00:06:59.530 --> 00:07:06.209
There are scenes and shots that are identical to their theatrical versions, including Foley, score, and colour grade,

00:07:06.220 --> 00:07:12.029
but these are right next to scenes that are ungraded, incomplete, with temp effects and score.

00:07:12.200 --> 00:07:17.800
And, remember, this version was a mere three months before the film was released.

00:07:18.000 --> 00:07:23.190
A version of Justice League from over six months before release is going to be even more extreme,

00:07:23.680 --> 00:07:27.600
especially since there were segments Snyder was already planning to reshoot,

00:07:27.600 --> 00:07:29.600
or do extra photography for.

00:07:29.800 --> 00:07:33.600
So, when Justice League went into 8 weeks of additional photography,

00:07:33.910 --> 00:07:39.600
there were three possible outcomes for any given scene that Snyder already planned to reshoot:

00:07:39.800 --> 00:07:42.800
one, the scene was shot according to his instructions;

00:07:43.030 --> 00:07:47.000
two, the scene was shot, but changed from his plan;

00:07:47.200 --> 00:07:50.200
or three, the scene was never shot at all.

00:07:50.200 --> 00:07:55.200
That leaves a lot of potential holes in any possible Snyder cut, including scenes that now

00:07:55.479 --> 00:07:57.479
literally just don't exist.

00:07:57.580 --> 00:08:02.800
So, now let's take a bit to talk about what reshoots and additional photography are,

00:08:03.000 --> 00:08:06.800
because they're a thing that's been in a much higher public profile in the last few years,

00:08:06.800 --> 00:08:10.800
following a series of films that underwent extensive reshoots late in production,

00:08:11.000 --> 00:08:17.800
and this includes Josh Trank's Fantastic Four, David Ayer's Suicide Squad, Gareth Edwards' Rogue One.

00:08:18.000 --> 00:08:22.410
Modern, effects-heavy films all have a phase of additional photography and reshoots.

00:08:22.410 --> 00:08:24.700
It's a very normal thing.

00:08:24.910 --> 00:08:31.800
The movies are so big and complex with so much that involves actors working opposite tennis balls in an empty greenroom

00:08:32.000 --> 00:08:36.700
that it's inevitable that some of it just isn't going to stitch together.

00:08:36.909 --> 00:08:41.039
Typical, routine, boring reshoots consist of correcting eye lines,

00:08:41.140 --> 00:08:45.059
maybe redoing a few lines or a bit of action to make it all flow.

00:08:45.100 --> 00:08:51.700
Maybe a subplot is dropped during editing and a new minute or two of dialogue is written to patch the gaps left behind,

00:08:51.900 --> 00:08:55.289
or test audiences don't understand why a thing happens

00:08:55.440 --> 00:08:58.049
so a quick insert is created to make it more obvious.

00:08:58.390 --> 00:09:02.640
Or maybe in the case of Suicide Squad, in addition to being six hours long,

00:09:02.640 --> 00:09:06.390
the producer decides that Steppenwolf is now going to be the villain of Justice League,

00:09:06.520 --> 00:09:12.100
so you now need to rewrite and reshoot everything in your movie that directly involves the antagonists.

00:09:12.380 --> 00:09:18.400
These more dramatic instances in recent years are films that are in some state, real or imagined,

00:09:18.600 --> 00:09:23.100
that the studio considers to be dire, whether they consider it to be unmarketable,

00:09:23.100 --> 00:09:25.800
or possibly actively bad.

00:09:25.800 --> 00:09:28.570
The released version of Fantastic Four is abysmal,

00:09:29.020 --> 00:09:33.400
but the Trank cut was probably not in and of itself any better,

00:09:33.400 --> 00:09:37.000
because the Trank footage... isn't good.

00:09:37.200 --> 00:09:43.780
It's worth noting here that while the theatrical cut of Justice League is substantially different from the state of the film when Snyder left,

00:09:43.780 --> 00:09:47.500
no one has disputed that the core plot remained untouched.

00:09:47.500 --> 00:09:51.189
I mean, it's still a sequel to Batman v Superman.

00:09:51.190 --> 00:09:55.500
There are still the fundamental problems with the way that Snyder tells a story

00:09:55.500 --> 00:09:58.880
that were unlikely to just suddenly disappear,

00:09:58.880 --> 00:10:03.600
especially when Snyder himself has talked a lot about how much dropped footage

00:10:03.820 --> 00:10:06.200
was setups for later movies.

00:10:06.200 --> 00:10:12.300
What I'm saying is the Snyder Cut almost certainly had very real problems.

00:10:12.320 --> 00:10:16.500
The next critical factor is the substantial change in planned runtime.

00:10:16.700 --> 00:10:21.600
As of August 2017, Warners were telling film buyers to expect a 170 minute runtime,

00:10:21.800 --> 00:10:26.800
a length that was cut by almost an hour down to 118 minutes in October.

00:10:27.000 --> 00:10:30.219
Even a 170 minute version of the film is still

00:10:30.560 --> 00:10:35.000
44 minutes shorter than the 10-reel director's cut photographed for Vero.

00:10:35.000 --> 00:10:39.400
That 10-reel version was never going to go to audiences.

00:10:39.600 --> 00:10:44.980
The economics of theatrical distribution are pretty hostile to films over three hours long,

00:10:45.440 --> 00:10:50.859
even during the aughts. While Lord of the Rings was riding high, and movie runtimes definitely crept upwards,

00:10:51.380 --> 00:10:56.200
blockbuster films like The Dark Knight and Watchmen still came in under three hours.

00:10:56.630 --> 00:11:01.200
Exhibitors really aren't super fond of any movie over two and a half hours,

00:11:01.200 --> 00:11:06.200
because that's getting into the range where there's not a lot of time to turn over the theatre between shows

00:11:06.400 --> 00:11:10.000
if you still want to fit in three showings per screen per night,

00:11:10.200 --> 00:11:12.500
and once you're at three hours,

00:11:12.500 --> 00:11:18.429
it's basically impossible to do three showings without paying staff to stay until 3:00 a.m.,

00:11:18.620 --> 00:11:23.400
which is really not worth it unless it's a film that can guarantee butts in seats.

00:11:23.690 --> 00:11:27.300
Even in 2019, shady-ass corporation Disney,

00:11:27.400 --> 00:11:30.400
who are actively screwing over exhibitors at every turn,

00:11:30.600 --> 00:11:34.589
were worried about the 3 hour, 9 minute runtime of Avengers: Endgame,

00:11:34.750 --> 00:11:38.900
and that was the sequel to the fourth highest-grossing film of all time.

00:11:39.160 --> 00:11:45.400
So unless Zack Snyder is incredibly bad at his job and completely oblivious to the economics of making

00:11:45.600 --> 00:11:49.500
and releasing films, he wasn't done editing his movie.

00:11:49.500 --> 00:11:52.500
That's just a practical reality of the job.

00:11:52.700 --> 00:11:56.800
These issues: the movie being visually incomplete at the time Snyder left the project,

00:11:57.070 --> 00:12:02.070
planned footage that was never shot, and a work print runtime that was never going to go to theatres,

00:12:02.070 --> 00:12:05.939
this gives us a pretty good idea of what the Snyder Cut would look like.

00:12:06.160 --> 00:12:13.600
It's a work print full, of unfinished VFX, overly long scenes, bloated side content, and missing connective tissue.

00:12:13.800 --> 00:12:18.180
And, like Fantastic Four, or even just Batman v Superman before it,

00:12:18.370 --> 00:12:21.400
It was probably not actually any good.

00:12:21.400 --> 00:12:23.800
By the accounts that have trickled out over the past couple years,

00:12:24.040 --> 00:12:26.790
reports of characters and scenes that were cut entirely,

00:12:26.949 --> 00:12:33.400
it was probably in a very similar state to Batman v Superman, and the pre-reshoots version of Suicide Squad:

00:12:33.820 --> 00:12:39.400
bloated, unfocused, overly long, pointlessly grim, and just not particularly good.

00:12:39.519 --> 00:12:46.800
The list of cut characters, including Atom, Elinore Stone, Nuidis Vulko, Darkseid, Desaad, Martian Manhunter, and Heggra,

00:12:46.800 --> 00:12:51.419
points to a movie suffering from all the same problems as the films that preceded it:

00:12:51.970 --> 00:12:56.800
overstuffed with setup and cameos at the expense of pacing, payoff, and plot.

00:12:56.800 --> 00:12:59.500
Now, despite all those very likely problems

00:12:59.730 --> 00:13:03.750
I would actually be really interested in seeing the Snyder Cut,

00:13:04.300 --> 00:13:06.800
especially a work print version of the film.

00:13:06.800 --> 00:13:10.900
I have no problem watching an incomplete movie where 10 minutes are just

00:13:10.900 --> 00:13:16.600
storyboards or previous comps of a t-pose Superman flying at a static image of Steppenwolf,

00:13:16.600 --> 00:13:20.600
or actors in a green room wearing motion-capture pajamas.

00:13:20.800 --> 00:13:26.000
If anything, I think a work print would be more Interesting than a finished version

00:13:26.200 --> 00:13:29.600
It is, however, unlikely to happen anytime soon;

00:13:29.600 --> 00:13:33.200
not impossible, but really, really unlikely.

00:13:33.400 --> 00:13:39.000
The biggest problem is that Warners, and Snyder himself, are unlikely to be willing to release a work print,

00:13:39.200 --> 00:13:43.800
never mind that there might be substantial complications with various guild and union contracts,

00:13:44.000 --> 00:13:48.500
stemming from commercially releasing a work print when a theatrical version exists.

00:13:48.700 --> 00:13:55.000
Now, that part can probably be sidestepped by releasing it as a "bonus feature" with a special edition Blu-ray,

00:13:55.000 --> 00:13:57.800
so you're not technically just selling the work print,

00:13:58.000 --> 00:14:02.370
but I, I don't know, because no one to my knowledge has ever done it,

00:14:02.800 --> 00:14:05.000
especially not for a movie of this scope.

00:14:05.000 --> 00:14:12.200
But, springing from that problem, that Warners and Snyder don't want to release an incomplete movie, is the cost:

00:14:12.610 --> 00:14:17.400
VFX experts have estimated that the combined cost of finishing scenes that weren't completed

00:14:17.500 --> 00:14:20.200
and redoing scenes that were changed

00:14:20.300 --> 00:14:23.000
would probably cost another 30 to 40 million dollars,

00:14:23.600 --> 00:14:27.600
and it's a big question mark as to whether or not that would be worth it

00:14:27.700 --> 00:14:33.200
when the result is unlikely to draw mass attention or even substantially improve the end product.

00:14:33.200 --> 00:14:36.100
The last and biggest hurdle is ego:

00:14:36.310 --> 00:14:38.310
Warner Bros. just doesn't want to,

00:14:38.350 --> 00:14:42.200
because they hold all the cards and don't want to look like they caved

00:14:42.200 --> 00:14:45.800
or made the wrong choice when they cut Snyder out and reworked the movie.

00:14:45.800 --> 00:14:51.200
From a political perspective, it is seemingly a lose-lose for Warner Bros.

00:14:51.400 --> 00:14:55.700
Like, okay, either the Snyder Cut sucks just as bad as Batman v Superman,

00:14:55.959 --> 00:15:00.600
being the same bloated, incoherent mess as Snyder's other two Superman movies,

00:15:00.800 --> 00:15:02.600
or it's amazing.

00:15:02.600 --> 00:15:07.700
If the movie isn't particularly good or it's even only kind of slightly better,

00:15:07.700 --> 00:15:10.499
then they look like suckers for spending tens of millions of dollars

00:15:10.720 --> 00:15:15.800
finishing a movie that only the most hardcore fans and cinephiles will care about.

00:15:15.800 --> 00:15:17.800
If they finish and release the Snyder Cut and it's amazing,

00:15:18.089 --> 00:15:20.700
then they look like the dumbasses who ruined a movie,

00:15:20.700 --> 00:15:23.400
and the people who made that decision then get fired,

00:15:23.600 --> 00:15:29.789
and the people who would get fired are the same people who currently get to decide if it ever gets released.

00:15:30.250 --> 00:15:33.600
So, Warner Bros. doesn't have a whole lot of incentive to release it,

00:15:33.600 --> 00:15:39.569
either as a work print or as a finished movie, and this is almost certainly why it didn't surface as an

00:15:39.570 --> 00:15:44.800
extended edition, director's cut, or even in chunks of bonus features on the home video release.

00:15:45.000 --> 00:15:51.000
All that's unlikely to change without substantial internal changes in priorities or leadership.

00:15:51.400 --> 00:15:54.600
However, those changes may have already happened.

00:15:54.880 --> 00:16:01.619
There are rumours that began circulating in November 2019 that the Snyder Cut of Justice League will be available on Warner

00:16:01.620 --> 00:16:06.200
Media's HBO Max streaming service when the service launches in May 2020.

00:16:06.400 --> 00:16:11.100
Using the cut as a teaser to draw subscribers to a subscription platform

00:16:11.100 --> 00:16:13.600
rather than selling it directly on home video

00:16:13.839 --> 00:16:19.619
might just provide enough layers of politically convenient cover for Warners to feel like it's a win,

00:16:19.800 --> 00:16:25.000
especially since the margins on a subscription service are much higher than on a single Blu-ray.

00:16:25.000 --> 00:16:28.000
The most persuasive argument supporting the rumours is

00:16:28.000 --> 00:16:30.600
that on the two-year anniversary of the film's release,

00:16:30.800 --> 00:16:35.200
Gal Gadot, Ben Affleck, and Jason Momoa posted the hashtag on social media.

00:16:35.400 --> 00:16:41.200
Now, the reason that's persuasive is the Gal Gadot and Ben Affleck don't tweet.

00:16:41.200 --> 00:16:43.500
Their Twitter accounts are run by their publicists.

00:16:43.500 --> 00:16:49.600
There's functionally no personal content, in contrast to Jason Momoa, whose Instagram is full of personal stuff,

00:16:49.700 --> 00:16:53.729
from selfies to political statements defending the land rights of Native Hawaiians.

00:16:53.769 --> 00:16:59.800
The point is that Affleck and Gadot are unlikely to have just tweeted the hashtag for fun,

00:17:00.000 --> 00:17:04.000
and it is reasonable to assume it's part of a promotional campaign.

00:17:04.000 --> 00:17:06.669
These rumours, vaguely substantiated,

00:17:06.669 --> 00:17:13.259
insinuate the Snyder self-funded the completion of a cut of Justice League, and is at present in negotiation with Warners

00:17:13.569 --> 00:17:15.360
but, rumours being rumours,

00:17:15.360 --> 00:17:19.000
there are also conflicting reports that the version that might maybe

00:17:19.209 --> 00:17:23.200
possibly be released to HBO Max might in fact be a work print;

00:17:23.400 --> 00:17:29.790
a rumour seemingly supported by Snyder posting numerous screen-grabs of incomplete effects shots to his Vero account.

00:17:29.790 --> 00:17:32.600
It's not much and I wouldn't normally include it,

00:17:32.600 --> 00:17:35.900
but the rumours are an interesting narrative in and of themselves,

00:17:36.190 --> 00:17:40.500
regardless of whether or not the Snyder Cut does surface on HBO Max.

00:17:40.700 --> 00:17:45.510
This possibility actually presents a really interesting third outcome to all of this.

00:17:45.760 --> 00:17:49.020
If Snyder has self-funded completion of his version of the film,

00:17:49.020 --> 00:17:53.400
it would still be a compromised version, owing to scenes that remain unfilmed,

00:17:53.600 --> 00:17:57.400
the lesser resources available when self-funding the finished work,

00:17:57.400 --> 00:18:02.200
and it will remain a hilarious setup for now-cancelled sequels and spin-offs,

00:18:02.440 --> 00:18:08.000
but it will probably at least be a really interesting comparison.

